“Grounded” has a nice stack of meanings, and they rhyme with each other.

It can mean physically connected to the ground; electrically grounded, giving charge a safe path to earth; psychologically grounded, stable and oriented to reality; epistemically grounded, supported by evidence or first principles; aircraft grounded, prevented from flying; a child grounded, restricted from going out; an idea grounded in something, rooted in a basis or cause; or even, in machinery, something that has been ground down by abrasion.

What’s interesting is that several meanings share the same hidden structure: connection to a reference surface or constraint.

So “you’re grounded” can imply either:

\text{stability through connection}

or

\text{loss of freedom through constraint}

Same word, nearly opposite experiential meanings.

And electrical grounding adds another useful one: a place for excess energy to go without destroying the system.

So when I said “enough ground contact,” that can carry all three at once: reality reference, control surface, and safe discharge path.